{"conjecture": "Long-running software systems degrade not because individual changes are incorrect, but because each locally correct change is selected by tests that only encode *current* requirements, causing the system's implicit invariant structure to drift without any single change triggering a signal. Over time, the surviving implicit invariants—never deliberately designed, only incidentally preserved—form a load-bearing shadow architecture that constrains future changes, and this constraint is invisible to any local review process because no existing test covers the now-essential but never-specified relationships."}
